Z||000012||BN Boomer||02-08-2001||10:04 AM||||Well, it's not exactly cheap, but I got the Auto Xray EZ-Link Pro-Pack. This includes the EZ-Link scanner with the software and cables to scan OBD-1 GM, Ford, and Chrysler vehicles as well as all generic OBD-II vehicles. It cost me $359 at Schucks Auto Parts.<P>You can also get scanners with the software and cable for each of the specific applications. The GM OBD-1 scanner for 1982-1995 vehicles costs about $170.<P>I got the Pro-Pack because I needed the OBD-1 and OBD-II capablities and it made more financial sense to buy it instead of the individual kits.<P>You can check them out for yourself at <A HREF="http://www.autoxray.com/scanners.htm" TARGET=_blank>Auto Xray Scanners</A><P>My only other suggestion is to get one of those cheapo scan tools, which really amount to nothing more than a $30 paper clip. Frankly, I don't see the point of bothering with them. You might as well save the money and count how many times your SES light flashes when jumping the terminals rather than doing the exact same thing by counting how many times the LED flashes on one of those so-called "scan tools".||||reg||14
